WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
The Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture is an industrial architecture model that

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.910
organizes systems according to the functions they perform,

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:10.555
from the physical process through control and operations to enterprise planning.

00:00:10.555 --> 00:00:11.730
In security discussions,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.680
the term Purdue Model usually refers to this layered view of an industrial environment.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.755
The model gives engineers, operators, architects, and security teams a shared vocabulary.

00:00:20.755 --> 00:00:23.790
Instead of describing a network only by subnets or vendor products,

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.870
teams can discuss where a function belongs, what it depends on,

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.840
and how close it is to physical control.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.540
A controller at Level 1 has a different operational purpose and consequence than an

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.076
enterprise application at Level 4.

00:00:34.076 --> 00:00:37.630
OT security practitioners use the model to reason about trust boundaries,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.975
communication paths, access, monitoring, and incident containment.

00:00:40.975 --> 00:00:43.200
It helps expose questionable architecture.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:43.805
For example,

00:00:43.805 --> 00:00:47.760
a user workstation should not normally communicate directly with a field controller simply

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.400
because both devices use Ethernet.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.800
The Purdue Model is not a product, a protocol, or a complete security design.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.810
It does not prescribe every firewall rule,

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.110
and assigning a system to a level does not make that system secure.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.260
The model must be combined with asset inventory, current diagrams,

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.680
operational requirements, risk analysis, and engineering judgment.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.120
Modern environments also include cloud analytics, wireless devices, vendor platforms,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.210
remote operations,

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:16.120
and industrial Internet of Things systems that may not fit neatly into a simple stack.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.490
Use Purdue as a reference for asking better questions,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.080
not as a perfect map that forces every architecture into identical boxes.
